  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe primary goal is to minimize the evaluation function: `2*n*max(convolve(a,a, mode='full'))/(sum(a))^2`, where `a` is a non-negative sequence. The key observation is that while the previous program's approach used sophisticated convex optimization techniques, we can achieve lower scores by incorporating principles from phase retrieval and equalization theory. The evaluation function's L2-invariant (sum of squares) suggests a connection to spectra, so we will search sequences with balanced power spectra. We will flatten the autocorrelation envelope through regularization, then refine using gradient steps with analytic differentiation. This approach targets a smoother, more distributed sequence which provides better attack surface for minimization. Time budget is 1500 seconds.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe formulate the optimization over 800-length sequences. Let `a` be the coefficient vector. We require:\n   - Non-negative coefficients\n   - Sum to 1\n   - Spectrum of coefficients is nearly constant across frequencies to minimize convolution peaks\n\nWe create synthetic initial sequences based on:\n   - Fourier-normalization: sequences that are structured based on low-frequency modes\n   - Random walks: stochastic initialization for diversity\n\nThe core implementation will use:\n   - A regularization parameter to control spectral flatness\n   - Analytic computation of the gradient for efficiency\n   - Maintained non-negativity and simplex projections\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nWe maintain a three-phase strategy:\n\n- Phase1: Fourier-normal initialization\n   a. Generate 48 sequences via multi-index Fourier modes\n   b. Apply randomized rounding to minimize impact\n   c. Normalize to sum=1\n\n- Phase2: Randomized base and gradient search\n   a. Maintain a population of 5 candidate sequences\n   b. For each sequence, run gradient iterations:\n        i. Compute gradient of the evaluation function\n        ii. Project gradient via simplex\n        iii. Clamp coefficients to [0,1000] dynamically\n   c. Inject random cosine harmonics into each sequence\n\n- Phase3: Global optimization\n   a. Cluster all candidate sequences\n   b. Select best performer by evaluation metric\n   c. Final polish using simplex projection\n   d. Ensure all sequences are non-negative via post-processing\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nUse exponential cooling schedules for gradient steps. Target sequence length: 800. \nKey hyperparameters:\n   - Drop largest coefficient by 0.7x per iteration\n   - Maintain minimum coefficient count \u22650.05 (as fraction)\n   - Apply 3 Laplacian smoothers per sequence\n\n5. Budget and robustness\nTotal time: 1500 seconds\n- Phase1: 400 seconds\n- Phase2: 700 seconds\n- Phase3: 400 seconds\n\nWe include fallback for negative-sum candidates and memory management. The code uses:\n\n   - NaN checks with `np.isfinite`\n   - Initial length 800 (can adjust dynamically)\n   - Fancy indexing should note cast to float first\n\n6. Validation and implementation\nFinal candidate undergoes resampling to 800-length sequences. The evaluation function uses:\n   - `np.convolve` with mode='full'\n   - Strict math: `max(x for x in convolve(a,a))`\n   - Sum in float without finite check first\n\nAll coefficients are mathematically verified and repaired via:\n\n   - Def: `np.clip(a, 0, None)`\n   - Sum normalized: `a = a/np.sum(a)` if np.sum(a)>=0.01\n\nThe implementation follows standardized steps ensuring correctness within the evaluation sphere.",
